The Coupon Verification Controller is responsible for verifying the validity of a coupon. It checks if the coupon is active, if it has expired, and if it is applicable to the current user and order. Here is a simple implementation of the Coupon Verification Controller in PHP:

```php
<?php
defined('BASEPATH') or exit('No direct script access allowed');

class CouponVerification extends AdminController
{
    public function __construct()
    {
        parent::__construct();
        $this->load->model('coupons_model');
    }

    public function verify_coupon($code)
    {
        $coupon = $this->coupons_model->get_coupon_by_code($code);

        if (!$coupon) {
            // Coupon does not exist
            return false;
        }

        if ($coupon->valid_to < date('Y-m-d H:i:s')) {
            // Coupon has expired
            return false;
        }

        if ($coupon->for_new_users && !$this->session->userdata('is_new_user')) {
            // Coupon is for new users only and the current user is not a new user
            return false;
        }

        if ($coupon->is_reusable && $this->coupons_model->is_coupon_reused($coupon->id, $this->session->userdata('user_id'))) {
            // Coupon is reusable and has already been used by the current user
            return false;
        }

        // Coupon is valid
        return true;
    }
}
```

This controller assumes that you have a `coupons_model` model that has methods to get a coupon by its code, check if a coupon has been reused by a user, and delete a coupon. You will need to implement these methods in your `coupons_model` model. Additionally, you will need to add a `is_new_user` field to your user session data to track if a user is new or not. Finally, you will need to add a `is_reusable` field to your coupons table to track if a coupon can be reused by a user.

Once you have implemented the `CouponVerification` controller and the necessary model methods, you can use it in your checkout process to verify the validity of a coupon before applying it to an order. Here is an example of how you might use it in your checkout process:

```php
